{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And Sarai was barren; she had no child.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say ״she had no child״? And our Sages of blessed memory said (Yevamot 64a) that she was an aylonit, as it is stated ״she had no child.״ Now it appears to explain according to what they said there in Yevamot page 64, and this is their language: Rabbi Ami said, Abraham and Sarah were tumtumin, as it is stated (Isaiah 51:1) ״Look to the rock from which you were hewn and to the quarry from which you were dug.״ And Rashi explained: ״you were hewn״ — maleness was made for them; ״you were dug״ — femaleness was made for them, etc. — thus far. And they further said there: If a man married a woman and stayed with her ten years and she did not give birth — he must divorce her and give her a ketubah, etc. A proof for this matter is (Genesis 16:3) ״at the end of ten years of Abram's dwelling in the land,״ etc. And shall he derive it from Isaac? — Isaac was barren. If so, then Abraham too was barren? — that is what he should have asked, etc. — thus far. And the Tosafot wrote that the barrenness stated here is the tumtum that Rabbi Ami spoke of; and although from the plain sense it does not follow according to Rabbi Yehudah's position, who said that a tumtum that was torn open cannot beget offspring — and furthermore even according to the Rabbis it is not common that he would beget — thus far. Now according to the words of the Tosafot, it stands to reason that Sarah too was torn open before she was married. According to this, the verse is properly reconciled, for since both of them were tumtumin and were torn open, and according to Rabbi Yehudah they cannot beget, the verse was concerned lest the listener understand that what it stated ״And Sarai was barren״ was only because she was a tumtumet — therefore it states this, and the intention is that since she was a tumtumet, for this reason she is presumed barren; therefore He revealed and stated <b>she had no child</b>, meaning that she was examined by conjugal relations after she was torn open and did not give birth to Abraham — this is what ״she had no child״ means. Examine this well." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that Rabbi Yehudah did not say that a tumtum that was torn open cannot beget offspring except regarding the male, but regarding the female — no, and it is common that she gives birth. And according to this, the measurement of the verse is thus: <b>And Sarai was barren</b> — and do not say that the barrenness of which the verse speaks is because she was a tumtumet like Abraham; that is not so, for the tumtum condition prevents childbirth only in the male and not in the female. And here the barrenness is simply that she did not give birth, and therefore it states ״she had no child.״" "<b>And according to the opinion of</b> the one who says she was an aylonit, the verse may be explained in this manner: <b>And Sarai was barren</b> — meaning, she was found to be barren, and the intention is that something was discovered in her that had not been recognizable before. You now see that besides her having been a tumtumet and having been torn open, barrenness was also recognized in her — for if it were only because she was a tumtumet, it would not be justified to say ״and she was״ in a new sense of becoming (for this was known before he married her; rather, she was examined and found not to have given birth). And consequently, why was it necessary to say ״she had no child״? Rather, surely to say that she was an aylonit."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase נבדקה על ידי זיקה means she was examined through marital relations; זיקה here in the sense of conjugal contact."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:30.923075494Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}